[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/fuxia"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B16"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Bronstein-Larsen Variation"]
[StudyName "Caro-Kann Defense. The Theory"]
[ChapterName "– Bronstein-Larsen Variation: 4…Nf6 5.Nxf6 gxf6"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4Aqycynk/elirGeaM"]
[UTCDate "2019.04.25"]
[UTCTime "17:59:14"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ (5. Ng3 { is very rare. }) 5... gxf6 { Bronstein-Larsen Variation. The idea is to get the open g-file for an attack. But the damaged pawn structure is a long-term liability. I don't recommend this line.
White should discourage Black from playing e5 now. c3 and Nf3 are two ways to do that. } { [%cal Ge7e5] } (5... exf6 { ➡ Tartakower Variation } 6. Bc4 { Forgacs Variation }) 6. c3 (6. Nf3 Bg4 { points out why Nf3 isn't played so often. } { [%cal Ge7e5] } 7. Bf4 { takes control over e5 again. And after an exchange, White's queen will be replaced with the rook now, preserving the control over the d-file. This also makes castling harder for Black. } { [%cal Ra1d1,Gf4b8] } 7... Nd7 { keeps the idea of e5 alive. } 8. h3 { This is an interesting moment: Black can go into a quiet game with Bh5 or Bxf3 now, or play Qa5+. } 8... Qa5+ { [%csl Ge5,Gh5][%cal Ga5e5,Ge5h5] } 9. Bd2 (9. c3 { is now almost forced. Bd2 gives Black a nice initiative. } 9... Bh5 { White will very likely not play g4 here. The simple reason is that White's king had no safe place afterwards. } 10. Be2 { And now Black can finally play e5. The engine even recommends it. But … castling first is probably easier to play. } 10... O-O-O { This looks a bit uncomfortable for White. } { [%cal Gd8d1] } (10... e5 { Stockfish's recommendation } 11. Bg3 O-O-O { transposes to the theory }) 11. Bg3 e5 12. O-O Rg8 { Black's rooks are now perfectly placed. And while the engine still says that White is better, it's hard to recommend a move. Re1, Qc2, even a4 are okay. } { [%cal Gg8g1,Gd8d1] } 13. b4 { might be a good multi-purpose move: it drives the queen away from the 5th rank (Qd5 is just asking for further attacks) and starts the typical pawn storm in positions with opposite castling. } (13. Nh4 { was played in Potrata - Schmitzer. It's not the best move, but it does lead to a more quiet game that is easier to play for White. } 13... Bxe2 14. Qxe2 exd4 15. cxd4 Qd5 16. Rac1 Nb6 17. Nf5 h5 18. h4 Rg4 { Potrata, Janko - Schmitzer, Klaus, 1-0, WC35/pr09, 2011, https://lichess.org/siwhYUl0 } (18... Qxf5 { doesn't really work: } 19. Rxc6+ bxc6 20. Qa6+ { [%csl Rc7,Rb8][%cal Rg3b8,Ra6c8] } 20... Kd7 21. Qxa7+ Ke8 22. Re1+ { [%csl Re7][%cal Re1e7,Ra7e7] } 22... Qe6 23. Rxe6+ { and with that, Black loses the queen } 23... fxe6 24. Qxb6 { White is completely winning here. The a-pawn is almost unstoppable. } { [%cal Ra2a8] })) 13... Qa3 { is a good, aggressive reply. White is better here — says Stockfish — but I'd say it's a very sharp position in which both sides can play for a win. } { [%cal Ga3c3] }) 9... Qh5 { Pinning the h-pawn. } { [%cal Gh5h1] } 10. Be2 (10. Rg1) 10... Rg8 { [%csl Bg2][%cal Gg8g2] } 11. Rg1 Bxf3 12. Bxf3 Qh4 { [%cal Gh4d4] }) (6. Bc4 { Threat: Qh5 } { [%cal Rd1h5,Bh5f7] } 6... Bf5 { Now Qh5 can be answered with Bg6. } { [%cal Gf5g6] } 7. Ne2 e6) 6... e5 { right away. This is almost never played, but clearly worth a try. I'll recommend this for now. } (6... Bf5 { Bf5 is the main move here. } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Rh4][%cal Rf3h4,Rh4f5] } 7... Nd7 8. Nh4 Bg6 { Hoping for an exchange to fix the pawn structure. :) } 9. Be2 e6) (6... h5 7. Bc4 { Pressing on the weakest square. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7] } 7... Nd7 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd7b6,Bb6c4] } 8. Qb3 { Forcing e6. Rh7 is playable, but it disconnects the rooks forever. } { [%csl Gh7][%cal Rc4f7,Rb3c4,Gh8h7,Bh7f7] } 8... e6 (8... Rh7 9. Nf3 { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rc4d3,Bd3h7] } 9... Nb6 { [%cal Gb6c4] } 10. Be2 Rg7 { [%csl Gg2][%cal Gg7g2] } 11. g3 { The engine thinks just castling short is okay here, but … with the open g-file for Black, this is a difficult decision to make when you can't play like Stockfish. }) 9. Nf3 Nb6 10. Be2 Qc7 11. O-O { White has enough defenders on the kingside to block any attack. After c4, the queen can also switch to the kingside.
Black has to castle long and then try to attack White's king per Rg8 and Bd6. But all of this slow, and White is already better developed. } { [%cal Rc3c4,Bb3h3,Gh8g8,Bg8g2,Gf8d6,Bd6h2,Gc8d7,Ge8c8] }) (6... Qd5 7. Be3 Bf5 { Main move. } (7... Rg8 { is maybe a bit too early. } 8. Nf3 Nd7 9. Qb3 Nb6 10. c4 Qh5 11. O-O-O Bh6 12. Re1 Be6 13. Qb4 O-O-O 14. b3 Rd7 15. Nd2 Rgd8 16. f4 Bg4 17. g3 e5 { Kagan, Shimon - Basman, Michael J, 0-1, Birmingham, 1977, https://lichess.org/yDngmztZ }) (7... h5 8. Qb3 Bh6 9. Nf3 Bxe3 10. fxe3 Qe4 11. Kd2 Nd7 12. Bc4 e6 { Godena, Michele - Short, Nigel D, 0-1, Reggio Emilia 53rd, 2011, https://lichess.org/y7MM2G9l }) 8. Ne2 e5 { Can and should now be played safely. } 9. Ng3 (9. dxe5 { is okay for Black: } 9... fxe5 10. Ng3 Qxd1+ 11. Rxd1 Be6 12. Bd3 Bxa2) 9... Be6 { [%cal Ge6a2] } 10. Qd2 (10. Qh5 Nd7 { Eggleston, D. - Pakleza, Z., 0-1, 6th Rethymno Open 2014, https://lichess.org/azJGc5iU }) 10... Nd7) 7. Nf3 (7. Bc4 Nd7 { Alonso, Salvador - Moreda, Lucas, 1-0, Mar del Plata op 40th, 2009, https://lichess.org/4jDxbmnz }) (7. dxe5 Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1 fxe5) 7... Qe7 8. Qc2 exd4+ 9. Be2 dxc3 10. O-O Rg8 { White is *still* better. } { [%cal Gc8h3] } (10... cxb2 { is probably too greedy. } 11. Bxb2 Be6 12. Rad1 Nd7 13. Nd4 Qc5 { ask for a queen trade that White should decline. } 14. Qd3 Bxa2) *


