1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 (3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 { Same position as in 3.Nc3. }) 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Bd3 { Alekhine Gambit. This is rarely played nowadays, and I don't recommend it. Sacrifices d4 with the idea to develop the white pieces with tempo: attacks on the queen. Black should accept nevertheless. } { [%csl Rd4] } 5... Qxd4 { A basic rule for Black here is: Piece trades are good for Black. This is now a Scandinavian in which Black is a pawn up. } 6. Nf3 { [%cal Rf3d4] } 6... Qd5 { Prevents Nge5. } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gd5g5,Re4g5] } (6... Qd8 { is also possible. But it might give White too much freedom in the center. } 7. Neg5 { is the critical idea for White } { [%cal Rg5f7,Rd1d8,Bd3c4,Bc4f7] } (7. Qe2 Nxe4 8. Bxe4 (8. Qxe4 Na6 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ga6c5,Bc5d3,Bc5e4] } 9. Be3 { [%csl Rc5][%cal Re3c5] } (9. Bxa6 { doesn't work: } 9... Qa5+ 10. Bd2 Qxa6) 9... Be6 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge6d5,Bd5e4] } 10. O-O Bd5 11. Qa4 { [%cal Rd3a6,Ra4a6] } 11... Nc7) 8... g6 9. O-O Bg7 { [%cal Gg7b2] } 10. c3 Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gd7f6,Gc5e4,Gf6e4] } 11. Bc2 e5 { Block squares for the knight and the bishop. } { [%csl Gf4,Gd4][%cal Rf3d4,Rc1f4] }) 7... Qc7 { is the best response. The queen has to step out of the unprotected square d8. } { [%cal Rd1d8] } (7... h6 { for example would lose the game immediately: } 8. Nxf7 { [%cal Gf7h8,Gf7d8] } 8... Kxf7 9. Bg6+ Kxg6 10. Qxd8)) 7. Qe2 (7. c4 Qd7) 7... Nxe4 8. Bxe4 Qh5 9. Be3 Bf5 10. Bxf5 Qxf5 11. O-O-O Nd7 { Black is fine here. White has very little compensation for the pawn. } *


[Event "Caro-Kann Defense. The Theory: Advance Variation Overview: 2.d4 d5 3.e5"]
[Date "2017.05.15"]
